WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.104 --> 00:00:16.591
For the next three weeks, Anne and Priscilla continued to feel like strangers in a strange land.

2
00:00:17.452 --> 00:00:23.635
But as they attended classes and social events, and became familiar with the professors and other students, Anne

3
00:00:23.655 --> 00:00:24.816
and Priscilla felt better.

4
00:00:25.748 --> 00:00:33.854
The first-year students, instead of being a collection of unrelated individuals, became close acquaintances who all attended class

5
00:00:33.914 --> 00:00:36.836
events and shared a class song and class spirit.

6
00:00:38.658 --> 00:00:41.159
Gilbert Blythe was elected class president.

7
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:47.004
He was also invited to join a fraternity, which was a rare honor for a first-year student.

8
00:00:48.076 --> 00:00:53.461
As an initiation to the fraternity, Gilbert had to walk down the main streets of Halifax for a

9
00:00:53.561 --> 00:00:57.164
whole day wearing a bonnet and a colorful kitchen apron.

10
00:00:58.145 --> 00:01:01.347
He did this cheerfully, shaking ladies' hands as they passed.

11
00:01:02.868 --> 00:01:07.212
Anne and Priscilla found themselves very involved in the social life of Redmond College.

12
00:01:08.193 --> 00:01:11.215
Philippa Gordon came from a rich and well-known family.

13
00:01:12.256 --> 00:01:16.820
This, along with her beauty and charm, got her invited to many parties and events.

14
00:01:17.704 --> 00:01:20.445
And wherever she went, Anne and Priscilla went too.

15
00:01:21.905 --> 00:01:26.466
It was a mystery to Anne how Phil found time to study because she was always going out,

16
00:01:26.827 --> 00:01:28.067
especially with young men.

17
00:01:29.807 --> 00:01:33.889
Nine-tenths of the male students in first year were rivals for Phil's smiles.

18
00:01:33.909 --> 00:01:42.571
"Your two special hometown 'friends', Alec and Alonzo, don't seem to have any serious rivals yet," remarked Anne

19
00:01:42.631 --> 00:01:46.372
teasingly. "Not one," agreed Philippa.

20
00:01:47.156 --> 00:01:49.237
"Of course, I can't get the boy I like best.

21
00:01:50.137 --> 00:01:53.839
Gilbert Blythe won't take any notice of me, except to look at me as if I'm a little

22
00:01:53.899 --> 00:01:55.020
kitten he'd like to pat.

23
00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:57.261
I know the reason, Anne.

24
00:01:58.041 --> 00:01:59.922
It's clear that Gilbert likes you.

25
00:01:59.942 --> 00:02:04.144
I really ought to hate you, but instead I adore you!

26
00:02:04.164 --> 00:02:08.145
Around you, I wish I was wiser and stronger.

27
00:02:08.165 --> 00:02:11.847
Anne, please tell me over again that you like me a little bit!"

28
00:02:11.867 --> 00:02:13.848
"I like you a lot!

29
00:02:14.732 --> 00:02:18.876
And I think you're a dear, sweet, adorable little kitten."

30
00:02:19.616 --> 00:02:20.217
Anne laughed.

31
00:02:20.237 --> 00:02:24.360
"But I don't understand how you ever have time to study!"

32
00:02:26.422 --> 00:02:31.405
Somehow Phil must have found time to study because she was the top student in every class except

33
00:02:31.486 --> 00:02:33.687
English, where Anne came first by far.

34
00:02:34.768 --> 00:02:39.452
Anne found her studies easy, leaving her time for a social life that she thoroughly enjoyed.

35
00:02:40.428 --> 00:02:45.030
But never for a moment did she forget Avonlea and her family and friends there.

36
00:02:47.511 --> 00:02:51.092
For Anne, the happiest time of each week was when she read letters from home.

37
00:02:52.053 --> 00:02:56.855
The first batch had contained letters from Ruby Gillis, Diana, Marilla, Mrs.

38
00:02:56.915 --> 00:03:00.736
Lynde, and Jane Andrews, who had taken over as the new schoolteacher.

39
00:03:01.777 --> 00:03:05.378
Jane's letter was neatly written, without an interesting sentence in it.

40
00:03:06.298 --> 00:03:09.800
She didn't answer any of the questions that Anne had asked in her letter to Jane.

41
00:03:11.648 --> 00:03:16.511
But Jane did tell Anne how many yards of lace she had crocheted and how she felt when

42
00:03:16.551 --> 00:03:17.292
her head ached.

43
00:03:18.413 --> 00:03:23.356
Ruby Gillis wrote about how much she was missing Anne and asked what the Redmond boys were like.

44
00:03:24.477 --> 00:03:28.920
Ruby's letter was silly and harmless, and Anne would have laughed except for one part.

45
00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:34.984
"Gilbert seems to be enjoying Redmond, judging from his letters," wrote Ruby.

46
00:03:35.004 --> 00:03:37.905
"I don't think Charlie likes it so much."

47
00:03:39.086 --> 00:03:41.288
So Gilbert was writing to Ruby!

48
00:03:42.412 --> 00:03:50.594
"Gilbert can write to anybody he wants to, of course," thought Anne, "but Anne was completely confused by

49
00:03:50.634 --> 00:03:51.274
her feelings.

50
00:03:52.275 --> 00:03:57.056
What Anne didn't know was that Ruby had written to Gilbert first and that he had simply answered

51
00:03:57.216 --> 00:03:57.896
to be polite.

52
00:03:59.737 --> 00:04:03.238
Marilla's letter was rather prim, with no gossip or emotion.

53
00:04:04.238 --> 00:04:08.359
Yet somehow it conveyed the wholesome, simple life at Green Gables.

54
00:04:09.339 --> 00:04:11.480
Mrs. Lynde's letter was full of church news.

55
00:04:13.188 --> 00:04:17.391
"I wonder," said Anne as she folded up her letters, "what Mrs.

56
00:04:17.491 --> 00:04:18.972
Lynde would think of Philippa."

57
00:04:20.333 --> 00:04:26.838
One fine winter day, Gilbert, Anne, Priscilla, Charlie, and Phil met to go for a walk through Halifax.

58
00:04:28.339 --> 00:04:31.041
"Let's go down Spofford Avenue," suggested Gilbert.

59
00:04:31.061 --> 00:04:34.683
"We can see all the beautiful houses where the rich people live.

60
00:04:35.504 --> 00:04:37.965
Nobody can live there unless he or she is a millionaire."

61
00:04:37.985 --> 00:04:40.147
"Oh, yes!"

62
00:04:40.707 --> 00:04:41.208
said Phil.

63
00:04:41.972 --> 00:04:45.173
"There's a perfectly gorgeous little place I want to show you, Anne.

64
00:04:46.094 --> 00:04:47.614
It wasn't built by a millionaire.

65
00:04:47.695 --> 00:04:52.437
I swear it must have grown out of the ground while Spofford Avenue was still a country road.

66
00:04:53.397 --> 00:04:56.658
I don't like the other houses there; they're too new and showy."

67
00:04:58.839 --> 00:05:01.340
They saw the small house as they walked up the hill.

68
00:05:02.161 --> 00:05:04.582
It was white, with pine trees on both sides.

69
00:05:05.622 --> 00:05:09.484
The whole place looked like it had been transplanted from some remote country village.

70
00:05:10.452 --> 00:05:14.554
"It's the sweetest place I've ever seen," said Anne, delighted.

71
00:05:14.574 --> 00:05:18.275
"It's even quainter than Miss Lavender's house."

72
00:05:20.736 --> 00:05:27.939
"Look at the name, Anne," said Phil, "in white letters around the archway over the gate: 'Patty's Place.'

73
00:05:28.779 --> 00:05:29.800
Isn't that gorgeous?"

74
00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:32.581
"Have you any idea who Patty is?"

75
00:05:33.061 --> 00:05:33.741
asked Priscilla.

76
00:05:33.761 --> 00:05:39.884
"Patty Spofford is the name of the old lady who owns it, I've discovered," said Phil.

77
00:05:40.636 --> 00:05:41.957
"She lives there with her niece."

78
00:05:43.559 --> 00:05:47.202
"I'm going to dream about Patty's Place tonight," said Anne.

79
00:05:47.222 --> 00:05:52.306
"I wonder if, by any chance, we'll ever see the inside of it."

80
00:05:52.326 --> 00:05:55.428
"It isn't likely," said Priscilla.

81
00:05:56.509 --> 00:05:58.031
Anne smiled mysteriously.

82
00:05:58.051 --> 00:06:03.035
"No, it isn't likely, but I believe it will happen.

83
00:06:03.976 --> 00:06:09.000
I have a strange feeling that Patty's Place and I are going to become better acquainted."
